    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Power_Transmission_Network\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    n_nodes = 5\n    nodes = list(range(n_nodes))\n    n_generators = 2\n\n    # Node coordinates (x, y) in km\n    node_coords = [(95, 89), (15, 57), (58, 80), (65, 26), (29, 28)]\n\n    # Node types\n    node_types = ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n\n    # Power demands and generator capacities (MW)\n    power_demands = [0.0, 0.0, 16.0, 25.0, 29.0]\n    generator_capacities = [78.0, 86.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]\n\n    # Wire physical properties (INDUCED PARAMETERS)\n    resistivity = 1.68e-08  # Ohm-meter\n    wire_area_mm2 = 240  # mm^2\n    wire_area_m2 = wire_area_mm2 * 1e-6  # Convert to m^2\n\n    # Operating parameters\n    operating_voltage = 500  # kV\n    line_capacity = 300  # MW\n    build_cost_per_km = 87501  # $/km\n    loss_cost_rate = 45  # $/MWh equivalent\n\n    # COMPUTE distance and resistance matrices (INDUCED COSTS)\n    # Using Ohm's Law: R = resistivity * length / area\n    def compute_distance_km(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    def compute_resistance(dist_km):\n        dist_m = dist_km * 1000  # Convert km to m\n        return resistivity * dist_m / wire_area_m2\n\n    distances = {}  # km\n    resistances = {}  # Ohm\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist = compute_distance_km(node_coords[i], node_coords[j])\n                distances[(i, j)] = dist\n                resistances[(i, j)] = compute_resistance(dist)\n\n    # Create edge list (undirected, i < j)\n    edges = [(i, j) for i in nodes for j in nodes if i < j]\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Build_i_j: Binary - whether to build line between nodes i and j\n    build = {}\n    for (i, j) in edges:\n        build[(i, j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Build_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Flow_i_j: Continuous - power flow from node i to j (MW)\n    flow = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                flow[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Helper function to get build variable (handles i > j case)\n    def get_build(i, j):\n        if i < j:\n            return build[(i, j)]\n        else:\n            return build[(j, i)]\n\n    # Objective: Minimize construction cost + operational transmission cost\n    # Cost factor: converts resistance to cost coefficient\n    # Cost = LossRate * (R / V^2) * P^2\n    # factor = LossRate / V^2\n    loss_factor = loss_cost_rate / (operating_voltage ** 2)\n\n    # Construction costs (based on distance)\n    obj = quicksum(distances[(i, j)] * build_cost_per_km * build[(i, j)] for (i, j) in edges)\n\n    # Operational costs: Quadratic (I^2*R losses)\n    # Note: Gurobi supports quadratic objectives directly\n    obj += quicksum(\n        resistances[(i, j)] * loss_factor * flow[(i, j)] * flow[(i, j)]\n        for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Flow conservation constraints\n    for node in nodes:\n        inflow = quicksum(flow[(other, node)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        outflow = quicksum(flow[(node, other)] for other in nodes if other != node)\n        \n        if node_types[node] == 'generator':\n            # Generator: net outflow (generation) <= capacity\n            # Rewritten: inflow - outflow >= -capacity\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= -generator_capacities[node],\n                name=f\"Gen_Cap_{node}\"\n            )\n        else:\n            # Consumer: net inflow >= demand\n            m.addConstr(\n                inflow - outflow >= power_demands[node],\n                name=f\"Demand_{node}\"\n            )\n\n    # Capacity constraints: Flow <= line_capacity * Build\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    flow[(i, j)] <= line_capacity * get_build(i, j),\n                    name=f\"Cap_{i}_{j}\"\n                )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Planning memo\u2014Transmission network design for a five\u2011node generator\u2013consumer system\n\nWe are preparing a build-out plan for a small transmission network that connects two generator nodes to three consumer nodes. The utility\u2019s challenge is to determine which lines to construct and how to route power so that all consumer demand is met, generator outputs stay within their limits, and total cost (construction plus the cost of resistive heating losses) is minimized. Power can only flow over lines we choose to build, and each line has an upper transfer capacity that cannot be exceeded.\n\nThe planning inputs available to us are:\n- Node locations: Node Coordinates (km):\n  - N0 (generator): (95, 89)\n  - N1 (generator): (15, 57)\n  - N2 (consumer): (58, 80)\n  - N3 (consumer): (65, 26)\n  - N4 (consumer): (29, 28)\n- Node roles (generator or consumer): ['generator', 'generator', 'consumer', 'consumer', 'consumer']\n- Consumer demand levels: Consumer demands: N2: 16.0 MW, N3: 25.0 MW, N4: 29.0 MW\n- Generator maximum outputs: Generator capacities: N0: 78.0 MW, N1: 86.0 MW\n- Copper conductor resistivity: 1.68e-08 \u03a9\u00b7m (copper)\n- Conductor cross\u2011section: 240 mm\u00b2\n- Network operating voltage: 500 kV\n- Maximum power per line: 300 MW\n- Construction cost per kilometer: $87,501/km\n- Cost rate for energy lost to heating: $45/MWh\n\nAt a high level, line construction cost scales with the physical distance between nodes, so topologies that require longer spans or additional branches will incur greater capital expense. At the same time, operational costs are driven by resistive losses in the conductors. These losses follow a square law with respect to power flow: double the power on a line and the heating losses increase fourfold. Physically, the loss on a given line is proportional to its resistance and to the square of current, and since current is power divided by voltage, the loss is well captured by the relationship \u201closs ~ resistance \u00d7 (power/voltage)\u00b2.\u201d This is why higher voltage operation reduces loss so effectively\u2014the current drops, and losses fall with the inverse square of voltage.\n\nLine resistance depends on the wire material and geometry. Using copper conductors, the resistance grows with line length and falls with larger cross\u2011section. In practical terms, longer routes and thinner conductors produce more heating for the same power flow, and the associated cost goes up steeply as flows increase. Because of this quadratic behavior, concentrating power on a single path can become expensive; splitting flows across multiple built lines can reduce heating costs, but doing so may add construction cost and must respect each line\u2019s capacity limit.\n\nFor planning purposes in this study, we will treat power flow as conserved at every node: the sum of power arriving at a node (plus local generation) equals the sum of power leaving the node (plus local demand). Losses are accounted for as a separate financial penalty; they do not reduce the delivered power in the nodal balances. Under this approach:\n- Consumers must receive their full demand.\n- Generators must operate within their maximum outputs.\n- Power can only move along lines we build.\n- Any line flow must not exceed its capacity limit.\n- The operating cost for losses uses the physics-based quadratic model above, with the loss cost rate converting energy lost to dollars.\n\nThe objective is to select which lines to construct and how to dispatch power through them so that the total of construction costs and the cost of resistive losses is minimized. A good solution will balance shorter distances (lower capital), adequate capacity, and the strong incentive to keep currents low (higher voltage helps, and avoiding excessive concentration of power on any single line reduces square-law heating costs). This formulation ensures we capture the real physics of transmission losses while keeping the nodal power balances lossless for planning clarity.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Planning memo \u2014 cost-minimizing copper transmission build-out connecting generators and consumers\n\nWe are planning a new transmission build to connect our supply and demand points across the service area. The map locations for all nodes are provided in {NODE_COORDINATES}, and each node\u2019s role (generator or consumer) is indicated in {NODE_TYPES}. The load we are required to serve is given in {POWER_DEMANDS}, and the available output at generator sites is limited by {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}. Our task is to choose which transmission lines to construct and how to route power so that all consumer demand is met at minimum total cost, recognizing that power can only flow on lines we actually build.\n\nLine design and costing assumptions:\n- Conductors are copper. The material property used for resistance calculations is specified in {WIRE_RESISTIVITY}, and the conductor size is defined by {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}. Resistance on any span grows with length and shrinks with thicker conductors, so longer or thinner lines incur higher electrical losses.\n- The network will operate at a nominal transmission voltage of {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}. Higher voltage is a primary lever for reducing losses because it lowers current for the same power transfer.\n- Each candidate corridor is subject to a maximum transferable power rating of {LINE_CAPACITY}, which caps the loading we can place on any single line.\n- Construction cost scales with route length at {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}. For planning, distances are taken from the geometry implied by the node coordinates.\n\nOperational loss and cost treatment:\n- Resistive losses follow the square law: heating on a line is proportional to its resistance times the square of the current. Since current equals power divided by voltage, the loss grows with Resistance \u00d7 (Power/Voltage)^2. Doubling the power on a given span leads to four times the resistive loss, which is why heavily loaded lines are disproportionately expensive to operate.\n- We monetize these losses at {LOSS_COST_RATE}. This makes the cost impact of loading decisions explicit and highlights the benefit of operating at higher voltage, which reduces loss via the 1/Voltage^2 relationship.\n- This is a planning model. We conserve power at every node\u2014losses are not subtracted from the nodal balances. Instead, we assess a financial penalty for the heating implied by the chosen flows. We will not use linear approximations or simply sum flows; the loss cost must reflect the physics-based square-law behavior.\n\nDecision problem and constraints:\n- Select which line segments to build between nodes. No power can flow on an unbuilt segment.\n- For the chosen topology, assign steady-state power flows so that all consumer nodes receive the required demand while each generator remains within its output limit.\n- Keep every built line within its transfer rating. Because loss cost grows with the square of flow, spreading power across multiple paths can reduce operating cost, but only if the added construction expense is justified by the savings. The model should therefore weigh shorter, higher-utilization corridors against longer or parallel routes that reduce heating.\n\nOutcome:\nWe seek the network design that minimizes the sum of distance-based construction cost and ongoing resistive heating cost, using the actual node locations and roles, copper conductor properties, operating voltage, and per-line capacity limits. By explicitly modeling the quadratic nature of resistive losses, the plan will capture the real trade-off between building more infrastructure and paying higher operating costs over time."
